Now I have a complete picture of the entire codebase. Let me trace the exact bug precisely.

The bug is now fully understood. Here is a precise, file-by-file explanation of every root cause and the exact changes needed to fix it.

---

## Root Cause Analysis

The bug is a **stale-state object-pool problem** that manifests across two files and one supporting pool class. When a decorative sign is deleted, its game object (a chat-bubble/label carrying the text `"Welcome"`) is returned to the pool. When a new Villager is then created and acquires an object from that same pool, it gets the recycled sign object — still carrying the old `text: 'Welcome'` field — because **nothing clears the object's state before it is reused**.

There are **three distinct, layered defects** that together cause the glitch:

---

### Bug 1 — `server/server.js`: `Pool.release()` does not reset object state

**File:** `server/server.js`  
**Class:** `Pool`  
**Method:** `release(obj)`

```js
// Lines 218–222 — current (broken) code
release(obj) {
  if (this.freeList.length < this.maxSize) {
    this.freeList.push(obj);   // ← pushed back with ALL old fields intact
  }
}
```

The `Pool` class is used to recycle chat-bubble/label objects (see line 225: `const chatBubblePool = new Pool(() => ({ id: uuid(), text: '', ttl: 0 }), MAX_CHAT_BUBBLES);`). When a sign's label object is released, its `text` field (e.g. `'Welcome'`) is **never cleared**. The next call to `acquire()` pops this dirty object off the free list and hands it to the new Villager entity, which then renders the stale text.

**Fix:** Reset the object's mutable fields inside `release()` before pushing it back onto the free list. Since the pool's factory defines the canonical "blank" shape `{ id, text, ttl }`, the reset should mirror that shape:

```js
// Fixed release()
release(obj) {
  if (this.freeList.length < this.maxSize) {
    // Reset all mutable fields to their factory-default values
    // before returning the object to the free list, so the next
    // acquire() never sees stale data from a previous owner.
    obj.text = '';
    obj.ttl  = 0;
    this.freeList.push(obj);
  }
}
```

> **Why not reset in `acquire()`?** Resetting on release is the safer convention: it guarantees the object is clean the moment it re-enters the pool, so any code path that acquires it (including warm-up, future callers, etc.) always gets a blank slate. Resetting only on acquire is fragile because it relies on every caller remembering to do so.

---

### Bug 2 — `public/src/ecs_engine.js`: `ObjectPool.release()` explicitly defers all responsibility to the caller, with no enforcement

**File:** `public/src/ecs_engine.js`  
**Class:** `ObjectPool` (the ECS-internal pool, lines 169–192)  
**Method:** `release(obj)`

```js
// Lines 184–187 — current (broken) code
release(obj) {
  // Highly generic; the caller is responsible for resetting object state.
  this._free.push(obj);
}
```

The comment "the caller is responsible for resetting object state" is the design intent, but it is **never enforced**. The `acquire()` method (lines 180–182) also does nothing to reset state:

```js
acquire() {
  return this._free.pop() || this.factory();
  // ← no reset call whatsoever
}
```

This means any game object (including a sign's label component) that is released into this pool and later acquired by a Villager creation path will carry all of its old component data.

**Fix:** Add an optional `reset` callback parameter to the constructor (mirroring the pattern used in `public/src/object_pool.js`'s `ChatBubblePool`), and call it inside `acquire()` so that every object is sanitised before being handed out:

```js
class ObjectPool {
  /**
   * @param {() => any} factory   Factory that returns a *new* instance
   * @param {number}    warmSize  How many objects to pre-allocate
   * @param {((obj: any) => void) | null} reset  Optional reset function called on every acquire
   */
  constructor(factory, warmSize = 0, reset = null) {
    this.factory = factory;
    this._reset  = reset;
    this._free   = [];
    while (this._free.length < warmSize) this._free.push(factory());
  }

  acquire() {
    const obj = this._free.pop() || this.factory();
    // Always reset before handing out, whether freshly created or recycled.
    if (this._reset) this._reset(obj);
    return obj;
  }

  release(obj) {
    // State is cleared on the next acquire(), so no stale data leaks
    // between owners even if the caller forgets to clean up.
    this._free.push(obj);
  }

  size() {
    return this._free.length;
  }
}
```

---

### Bug 3 — `public/src/object_pool.js`: `ChatBubblePool` `reset()` is only called when the caller passes arguments; a zero-argument `acquire()` leaves the bubble dirty

**File:** `public/src/object_pool.js`  
**Class:** `ObjectPool` (the standalone pool module)  
**Method:** `acquire(...resetArgs)` → delegates to `item.reset(...resetArgs)`

```js
// Lines 134–158 — current (broken) code
acquire(...resetArgs) {
  let item = this._stack.pop();
  // ...
  if (item.reset) {
    try { item.reset(...resetArgs); } catch (err) { ... }
  }
  // ...
  return item;
}
```

And the `ChatBubblePool`'s `reset` function (lines 282–285):

```js
reset(text = '', style = {}) {
  el.textContent = text;
  Object.assign(el.style, style);
},
```

If a caller does `ChatBubblePool.acquire()` with **no arguments**, `item.reset()` is still called — and `text` defaults to `''`, so this particular pool is actually safe *as long as `reset` is always called*. However, the `acquire()` method only calls `reset` **if `item.reset` is defined**. Any pooled object type that does **not** define a `reset` method (e.g. a plain game-object wrapper added later) will silently skip the reset step entirely, perpetuating the same class of bug.

**Fix:** Make the contract explicit and defensive. The `acquire()` method should **require** that pooled items define `reset`, or alternatively the pool constructor should accept a mandatory `reset` function (separate from the item itself) so the sanitisation contract cannot be accidentally omitted:

```js
// In the ObjectPool constructor, add a required `reset` option:
constructor({
  name,
  create,
  reset,          // ← NEW: mandatory reset function (obj, ...args) => void
  initial = 0,
  max = Infinity,
  autoGrow = true,
  collectStats = false,
}) {
  // ...
  if (typeof reset !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError(`[ObjectPool:${name}] "reset" must be a function. ` +
      `Provide a reset function to guarantee pooled objects are always clean on acquire().`);
  }
  this._resetFn = reset;
  // ...
}

acquire(...resetArgs) {
  let item = this._stack.pop();
  // ... (grow logic unchanged) ...

  // Always call the pool-level reset function — never rely on the item
  // having its own reset method, which can be forgotten.
  try {
    this._resetFn(item, ...resetArgs);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(`[ObjectPool:${this.name}] reset() failed:`, err);
  }

  this._stats && this._stats.acquired++;
  this._inUse.add(item);
  this._emit('acquire', item);
  return item;
}
```

Then update `ChatBubblePool` to pass `reset` at the pool level instead of on the item:

```js
export const ChatBubblePool = new ObjectPool({
  name   : 'ChatBubble',
  initial: 20,
  // Pool-level reset: always wipes text and style, regardless of call-site args.
  reset  : (el, text = '', style = {}) => {
    el.textContent = '';          // ← clear unconditionally first
    Object.assign(el.style, {});  // reset inline styles
    if (text)  el.textContent = text;
    if (style) Object.assign(el.style, style);
  },
  create : () => {
    const el = document.createElement('div');
    el.className = 'chat-bubble';
    el.style.position = 'absolute';
    el.style.pointerEvents = 'none';
    return el;   // no longer needs its own reset() method
  },
  max: 100,
});
```

---

## Summary Table

| File | Location | Problem | Fix |
|---|---|---|---|
| `server/server.js` | `Pool.release()` (line 218) | Object pushed back to free list with `text`, `ttl`, etc. still set from previous owner | Reset `obj.text = ''` and `obj.ttl = 0` inside `release()` before pushing |
| `public/src/ecs_engine.js` | `ObjectPool.release()` (line 184) and `ObjectPool.acquire()` (line 180) | Comment says "caller is responsible" but nothing enforces it; `acquire()` never resets | Add an optional `reset` callback to the constructor; call it inside `acquire()` on every object before returning it |
| `public/src/object_pool.js` | `ObjectPool.acquire()` (line 149) and `ChatBubblePool` definition (line 269) | `reset` is only called if the item defines it; no guarantee for future object types | Promote `reset` to a required pool-constructor parameter; call it unconditionally in `acquire()`, removing reliance on per-item `reset` methods |

The fundamental principle violated in all three cases is the same: **an object pool must guarantee a clean slate on every `acquire()`, not merely hope that callers or items remember to reset themselves.** The fix in each file enforces that guarantee at the pool boundary, making it impossible for a recycled sign's `"Welcome"` text to leak into a newly created Villager.
